"An attack tree decomposes an adversary goal into alternative or jointly required subgoals.\n\nUse when: Explore ways an attacker could reach a specific security outcome. Review whether mitigations address the modeled attack alternatives.\n\nAvoid when: Actual lateral movement between known assets is the primary subject.\n\nVisual grammar: The root names the undesired attacker outcome. Child nodes describe subgoals or concrete attack steps. AND and OR semantics are shown explicitly at each decomposition.\n\nBuild: Choose one attacker goal and state assumed capabilities. Break the goal into alternative approaches. Expand jointly required steps using explicit AND relationships. Lay out siblings with enough width for concise labels and keep gate symbols clear of connectors.\n\nCheck: Each child meaningfully contributes to its parent goal. AND and OR meanings are unambiguous. Feasibility, cost, or probability annotations have evidence or remain unknown. Tree edges and gate markers do not cross attack text.\n\nPitfalls: Assigning numerical risk values without defensible evidence. Treating the tree as exhaustive simply because every leaf fits on the page.\n\nExample: Obtain a session can occur through credential theft or a token leak; credential theft has its own prerequisite subgoals.\n\nAlternatives: Attack graph, Threat-model data-flow diagram."
